diagnosis_text:
The user's ticket accurately describes a past event: r8 restarted approximately 3 minutes before the diagnosis snapshot was taken (FRR log on r8 shows OSPF DD negotiations completing at 06:48:38 and 06:48:55). This restart temporarily brought down the OSPF virtual link (VLINK0) that connects area 0.0.0.2 to the backbone — the "invisible bridge" the user describes. Area 0.0.0.2 is r8's stub area (loopbacks 18.1.x.x/24 and eth1 80.0.0.0/8), which has no direct backbone path and depends entirely on this virtual link, traversing area 0.0.0.1 (transit) between r4 and r8. By the time diagnosis began, the virtual link had fully reconverged: both r4 (`show ip ospf neighbor` shows r8 via VLINK0, Full/DROther, adj=1, uptime 10m20s) and r8 (VLINK0 Full/DROther, adj=1, uptime 3m02s) confirm an active Full adjacency. The 7-minute uptime discrepancy is consistent with FRR Graceful Restart — r4 never detected a route withdrawal, and r3's OSPF table confirms area 0.0.0.2 prefixes (18.1.1–4.1/32, 80.0.0.0/8) have been continuously present for 10 minutes. All OSPF areas (0.0.0.0–0.0.0.4) are connected to the backbone, EIGRP adjacencies on r11 (to r12 and r13) are active, redistribution is working (115 OSPF route entries on r11), end-host reachability is 0% loss, and pressure_sweep shows all 15 devices clean. The virtual link collapse was a real but transient event that has self-recovered; no persistent fault remains.
=== END ===